## Contract to implement
`evidence/seam05.10/CONTRACT.md` is the map written before code. One command, `clerk-deliver`, in a new `app/clerk_seam.py`. The caller supplies an already queued and explicitly claimed job, the router link, the actor, the external fixture, and the container. The command does not queue, claim, or choose an actor, reviewer, route, or authority.

Clerk is reached only as a subprocess of the pinned `clerk.py`, after the hash matches. Job, checks, claim, and recorded return come from `show --json`. The seam does not read raw Clerk events and does not parse inbox text. While the job is claimed, `claim --resume` remains the public token contract: three lines, `resume`, `token`, and `instructions-usable sha256=`, and that token must equal the inspection token. Resume refuses a returned job, so a returned replay takes the token from inspection `claim.token` and still checks actor, instructions, and stored job identity.

The delivery is `worker_bridge.perform`. There is no second bridge, receipt validator, Clerk, queue, router, or intake registry. On every existing delivery path, including a returned replay, `link.bridge_job_sha256` must equal the original `provenance.job_sha256`, and the current job bytes must equal that same digest. Matching ids alone do not bind the historical request.

Uncollected, failed, and uncertain worker results stay out of a normal Clerk return. The seam stops, keeps the claim and the bridge evidence, and names the boundary. `bridge-recover` stays a separate command. An identical completed replay is read-only toward the worker: no launch. There is no cross-store transaction and no exactly-once claim. Interrupted copy or conflicting outbox bytes stay attention and are not overwritten or deleted.

The only worker backend the success path launches is an allowlisted protocol test worker. No model profile is configured. `run` stays refused.

